it's so it's good to remember that Yale  is a competitive place and as I  surprise when there was a sheaf of hand  rid of typed notes pulled out to the  side of me whereas I thought I would  speak somewhat extemporaneously and of  course to my surprise I'm seated between  two presidents  now you're making fun of us you'll pay  for that  so my background is engineering and I  went to what's now the School of  Engineering applied science and I have  to say that some of the comments made  earlier don't seem to necessarily apply  as much in engineering applied science I  was in the school from about nineteen  eighty to nineteen eighty four and in  engineering at least you tend to be  organized with research professors you  tend to see your fellow classmates or  research group members on a daily basis  in a lab or perhaps in preparing a lab  for students for teaching and there's  just a lot of daily interaction with  everybody so we're not kind of loan  asteroids wandering around the Yale  universe we are really to collect a  family and that that family was very  very important to me in terms of  learning how to study learning how to do  research and and that's really what I  think for me graduate school is all  about was kind of taking that next step  to teaching myself since then I was a  faculty member at Columbia I went to  work for the government for a while I  ran a couple of businesses i'm back in a  university environment that i was trying  to think what is special about the yale  graduate school and i started getting  worried because actually in the school  of engineering applied science we have  fairly at that time had fairly little  interaction with the graduate school I  remember coming in I remember depositing  my thesis and in between maybe a few  trips to hgs to visit some friends but  not too much in between but inside the  engineering school was a very strong  supportive environment which I really  appreciate and I was trying to think  what what was different about Yale  compared to my time at Columbia with my  students or my time at Dartmouth now or  my time in other endeavors and one of  the things that I thought about was  really special about Yale is that I  found you out to be a nurturing  environment rather  more Darwinian environment and what I  mean is that at Columbia for example I  don't mean to pick on Columbia but at  Columbia they would admit a lot of  graduate students in engineering of  course I'm talking about and then after  a year or so between rigorous coursework  and a very difficult examination they  would really weed out well that's  probably the wrong term they would  probably ask about half those students  to leave I don't remember the exact  statistics but it was a very large  fraction and their philosophy was well  you know if we put these students to  these tests and they survive they must  be really good where is it yet I found a  different environment which was that  over the course of the first year the  professor's took note of where your  areas of strengths were and where your  areas of weakness were and then after a  year they kind of sit down and talk with  you and try to strengthen those areas  where your week to try to make you kind  of strong across a wide range of areas  sometimes you can prescribing courses  you need to take that you didn't think  you had to take and so they're kind of  nurturing environment something that I  took with me beyond Yale of course I  tried to apply it amongst my own  graduate research group at Columbia but  also even going to a Jet Propulsion lab  and then in business I found that it  really is a very valuable skill to be  very careful about who you hire but once  they're in the door really try to  nurture them and find out what their  true skill sets are what they're good at  if they don't quite succeeding in the  first thing that you assign them to  maybe try to find something else now i'm  not that liberal I've certainly fired my  fair share of employees that didn't work  out but I think it's really worth while  to really take the time to try to find  out what people are good at and to put  them in the right position I think that  really counts a lot and so this I think  is really the strength that stands out  of my mind  my experience of the i'll graduate  school thank you
 

